\title{The Quadratic-Residue Lottery:\\ A Zero-Parameter Closed Form for the\\ Per-Target Sieve Footprint}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{4 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For a target integer $N$ and a factor base of odd primes $p \le B$, define the \emph{theory dial}
\[
T(N) \;=\; \sum_{\substack{p \le B \\ \Legendre{N}{p} = 1}} \frac{2}{p},
\]
the sum of $2/p$ over the factor-base primes modulo which $N$ is a quadratic residue. We prove that this expression, which contains no adjustable coefficient whatsoever, is \emph{exactly} the expected per-target sieve footprint of the factor base, and that no alternative linear read-out of the quadratic-residue indicator vector can match it. Specifically we establish: (i) an exact root-count identity $\#\{x \bmod p : x^2 \equiv N\} = \Legendre{N}{p} + 1$ identifying the counting function with the shifted Legendre symbol, whence the coefficient $2/p$ is forced; (ii) a \emph{sufficiency} statement --- the measured hit fraction at $p$ is a deterministic two-valued function of the single indicator bit, so no per-prime measurement can carry information beyond the bit; (iii) the exact distribution of the dial over the Chinese-remainder sample space: the indicator bits are exactly independent fair coins, every one of the $2^k$ patterns occurs equally often and is realised by an integer, the mean is the Mertens weight $\sum 1/p$ and the variance is exactly $\sum 1/p^2$; (iv) an exact risk formula for arbitrary fitted weight vectors, showing the theory weights $2/p$ are the \emph{unique} minimiser and pricing bit truncation at a strictly positive cost; and (v) two concentration theorems --- a Chebyshev bound $1/(2t^2)$ and a sub-Gaussian bound $2e^{-t^2}$ on the deviating fraction, both \emph{uniform in the factor base}, resting on the elementary estimate $\sum 1/q_i^2 < 1/2$ for distinct odd integers. A dual position-side analysis shows the same dial is the mean of the per-position factor-base hit counter, whose variance is $\sum_{\text{residue }p}(2/p)(1-2/p)$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

Congruence-of-squares factoring algorithms --- Dixon's method, the quadratic sieve, the multiple-polynomial quadratic sieve, and in modified form the number field sieve --- all rest on the same engine. To factor $N$, one scans an interval of positions $x$ and examines the polynomial values $f(x) = x^2 - N$, hoping that a sufficient number of them are \emph{smooth}: entirely composed of primes from a fixed factor base $\mathcal{B} = \{p : p \le B\}$. Each smooth value yields a relation, and once the relations outnumber the factor base, kernel vectors of the resulting $\mathbb{F}_2$-matrix produce congruences $u^2 \equiv v^2 \pmod N$ and, with probability at least one half, a nontrivial factor.

The throughput of such a run depends on the target $N$ in a way that is not uniform across targets. Some $N$ are hospitable to a given factor base and some are not; the practical question is whether one can \emph{predict}, cheaply and in advance, which is which. The natural predictive statistic is the per-target \emph{footprint} of the factor base: the expected number of factor-base divisions per sieve position. The purpose of this paper is to compute that footprint exactly, to determine its full distribution over targets, and to prove an optimality statement that forecloses the possibility of improving it by fitting.

\subsection{The main object}

Throughout, $p$ denotes an odd prime, $\Legendre{\cdot}{p}$ the Legendre symbol, and $\Sbase$ a finite set of primes.

\begin{definition}[Theory dial]
For a finite set $\Sbase$ of odd primes and an integer $N$,
\[
T_{\Sbase}(N) \;=\; \sum_{\substack{p \in \Sbase \\ N \text{ a nonzero square mod } p}} \frac{2}{p}.
\]
\end{definition}

The dial has no free parameters. It is determined by the factor base and by $k = |\Sbase|$ Legendre symbols, computable in $O(k \log^2 B)$ bit operations by quadratic reciprocity. The claim we prove is that this expression is not a model of the footprint but an identity for it.

\subsection{Empirical background}

The analysis was prompted by an empirical observation. Across two independent implementations, the dial evaluated over the primes $p \le 400$ correlated with measured sieve yield at Spearman rank correlations of $0.755$ and $0.7264$, and achieved out-of-sample coefficients of determination $R^2 = 0.541$ and $0.5335$ after fitting a \emph{single} global scale. A competing model with eight fitted per-bit coefficients reached only $R^2 = 0.463$; adding measured per-prime hit fractions on top of the indicator bits produced no improvement, and truncating the support of the weight vector degraded performance. Each of these findings has an exact mathematical counterpart below: sufficiency of the bits (Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficiency}), optimality of the theory weights (Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}), and the strict cost of truncation (Theorem~\ref{thm:truncpos}).

\section{Setting and notation}

Fix a factor base $\Sbase = \{q_1,\dots,q_k\}$ of \emph{distinct odd primes}.

For a prime $p$ and $N \in \Z$ put
\[
R(p,N) = \{x \in \{0,\dots,p-1\} : p \mid x^2 - N\}, \qquad r(p,N) = |R(p,N)|,
\]
so $R(p,N)$ is the set of residue classes of sieve positions at which $p$ divides $x^2 - N$. Define the indicator bit $b(p,N) = \mathbf{1}[r(p,N) > 0]$, the hit density $d(p,N) = r(p,N)/p$, the footprint $F_{\Sbase}(N) = \sum_{p \in \Sbase} d(p,N)$, and the counting character $\chi(p,N) = r(p,N) - 1$. Write $M(\Sbase) = \sum_{p \in \Sbase} 1/p$ for the Mertens weight and $X_{\Sbase}(N) = \sum_{p\in\Sbase} \chi(p,N)/p$ for the character fluctuation.

We call $\Sbase$ \emph{admissible for $N$} if every $p \in \Sbase$ is an odd prime with $p \nmid N$.

By the Chinese remainder theorem, residues of $N$ modulo $Q = q_1\cdots q_k$ coprime to $Q$ correspond bijectively to vectors in
\[
\Omega = \prod_{i=1}^{k} (\Z/q_i\Z)^{\times}, \qquad |\Omega| = \prod_{i=1}^k (q_i - 1),
\]
which we equip with the uniform measure; all sums over $\Omega$ are finite. Dually, $\Pi = \prod_i \Z/q_i\Z$ parametrises sieve positions modulo $Q$.

\section{The dial is an identity, not a model}

\subsection{The root count is the Legendre symbol}

\begin{theorem}[Root-count identity]\label{thm:rootcount}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $N \in \Z$. Then $r(p,N) = \Legendre{N}{p} + 1$, where the Legendre symbol is $0$ when $p \mid N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The count $r(p,N)$ equals the number of $x \in \Z/p\Z$ with $x^2 = \bar N$, since reduction mod $p$ is a bijection from $\{0,\dots,p-1\}$ onto $\Z/p\Z$. In a finite field of odd characteristic the squaring map is two-to-one on nonzero elements, so the fibre over $a$ has size $1 + \eta(a)$ with $\eta$ the quadratic character extended by $\eta(0)=0$; the fibre over $0$ is $\{0\}$, consistent with $1+0$. Identify $\eta$ with the Legendre symbol.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:trichotomy}
For an odd prime $p$ with $p \nmid N$: $r(p,N) = 2$ if $N$ is a square mod $p$ and $r(p,N) = 0$ otherwise; in particular $r(p,N) \in \{0,2\}$ with no intermediate value. If $p \mid N$ then $r(p,N) = 1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
The counting character $\chi(p,N) = r(p,N)-1$ coincides with the Legendre symbol for every odd prime $p$ and every $N$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The bit is a sufficient statistic}

\begin{theorem}[Lottery law; sufficiency of the indicator bit]\label{thm:sufficiency}
Let $p$ be an odd prime with $p \nmid N$. Then
\[
d(p,N) = \begin{cases} 2/p, & b(p,N) = 1,\\ 0, & b(p,N) = 0.\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Corollary~\ref{cor:trichotomy} and $d = r/p$.
\end{proof}

The measured hit fraction --- the quantity obtained by running the sieve and counting divisions by $p$ --- is a deterministic two-valued function of the single bit $b(p,N)$: no scatter, no residual, no third value. In the precise sense of statistical sufficiency, the bit vector $(b(q_1,N),\dots,b(q_k,N))$ carries all the information the per-prime measurements can carry, so no predictor consuming measured fractions in addition to the bits can add anything.

\subsection{The zero-fit theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-fit theorem]\label{thm:zerofit}
If $\Sbase$ is admissible for $N$ then $T_{\Sbase}(N) = F_{\Sbase}(N)$: the closed-form dial equals the expected footprint exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Sum Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficiency} over $p \in \Sbase$.
\end{proof}

The coefficient $2/p$ is therefore not fitted, not calibrated and not asymptotic; it is the unique value making the identity true, fixed by Theorem~\ref{thm:rootcount} before any data existed.

\begin{theorem}[Mertens decomposition]\label{thm:mertens}
For $\Sbase$ admissible for $N$,
\[
T_{\Sbase}(N) = M(\Sbase) + X_{\Sbase}(N) = \sum_{p \in \Sbase} \frac1p + \sum_{p\in\Sbase} \frac{\Legendre{N}{p}}{p}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:rootcount}, $d(p,N) = (1 + \chi(p,N))/p$ pointwise; sum and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:zerofit}.
\end{proof}

The decomposition separates a term independent of $N$ --- the Mertens weight, with $\sum_{p\le B} 1/p = \log\log B + M + o(1)$ --- from a Legendre character sum carrying all the $N$-dependence. In particular $0 \le T_{\Sbase}(N) \le 2M(\Sbase)$, both extremes attained (Theorem~\ref{thm:steer}).

\subsection{Removing the coprimality hypothesis}

\begin{definition}[Total dial]
$\widetilde T_{\Sbase}(N) = \sum_{p \in \Sbase} w_p$ where $w_p = 1/p$ if $p \mid N$, $w_p = 2/p$ if $p \nmid N$ and $b(p,N)=1$, and $w_p = 0$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Total exactness]
For any finite set $\Sbase$ of odd primes and any $N \in \Z$, $\widetilde T_{\Sbase}(N) = F_{\Sbase}(N)$; and $\widetilde T_{\Sbase}(N) = T_{\Sbase}(N)$ when $\Sbase$ is admissible for $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Case split on $p \mid N$, using $r(p,N)=1$ in the ramified case and Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficiency} otherwise.
\end{proof}

\section{The exact distribution of the dial}

Regard $T$ as a random variable on $\Omega$ under the uniform measure:
\[
T(x) = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \frac{2}{q_i}\,\mathbf{1}[x_i \text{ is a square}], \qquad x \in \Omega.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Fair lottery]\label{thm:fair}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and let $W_p$ (resp.\ $L_p$) be the set of $N \in \{0,\dots,p-1\}$ with $r(p,N)=2$ (resp.\ $0$). Then
\[
2|W_p| + 1 = p, \qquad |W_p| = |L_p| = \frac{p-1}{2}, \qquad \frac{|W_p|}{p-1} = \frac12 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Summing $r(p,\cdot)$ over all $p$ classes counts each $x \in \Z/p\Z$ exactly once, as a root of the unique class $x^2$; hence $\sum_{N=0}^{p-1} r(p,N) = p$. The class $N \equiv 0$ contributes $1$ and every other contributes $2$ or $0$, so $2|W_p|+1 = p$; with $|W_p|+|L_p| = p-1$ the ticket sets are equinumerous.
\end{proof}

Equivalently $\frac1p \sum_{N=0}^{p-1} d(p,N) = \frac1p$: each prime contributes on average exactly one expected hit per period, with no error term.

Write $\Win_i \subset (\Z/q_i\Z)^\times$ for the quadratic residues and $\Lose_i$ for the non-residues, so $|\Win_i| = |\Lose_i| = (q_i-1)/2$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact independence]\label{thm:indep}
For every pattern $\varepsilon \in \{0,1\}^k$,
\[
\#\{x \in \Omega : b(q_i,x_i) = \varepsilon_i \ \forall i\} = \prod_{i=1}^k \frac{q_i-1}{2} = \frac{|\Omega|}{2^k}.
\]
In particular the count is the same for all $2^k$ patterns, so the bit vector is uniform on $\{0,1\}^k$ and the bits are mutually independent fair coins.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The event is a product set whose factor cardinalities are $(q_i-1)/2$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:fair}, independently of $\varepsilon_i$.
\end{proof}

This is not a heuristic independence assumption of the kind common in sieve analysis but an exact combinatorial identity.

\begin{theorem}[Mean]\label{thm:mean}
$\E_\Omega[T] = \sum_{i=1}^k 1/q_i = M(\Sbase)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By linearity it suffices that $\E[\mathbf{1}[x_i \in \Win_i]] = |\Win_i|/(q_i-1) = 1/2$; formally, the one-coordinate marginal of a product set factors as the marginal sum times the number of completions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Variance]\label{thm:var}
$\Var_\Omega[T] = \sum_{i=1}^k 1/q_i^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $T - \E[T] = \sum_i c_i(x_i)$ with $c_i$ the centred coin taking values $\pm 1/q_i$. Expanding the square, diagonal terms give $\E[c_i^2] = 1/q_i^2$ and off-diagonal terms factor as $\E[c_i]\E[c_j] = 0$ by two-coordinate decoupling and the fairness of Theorem~\ref{thm:fair}.
\end{proof}

More generally, a read-out $\widehat T_w(x) = \sum_i w_i \mathbf{1}[x_i \in \Win_i]$ has mean $\sum_i w_i/2$ and variance $\sum_i w_i^2/4$.

\begin{theorem}[Steerability]\label{thm:steer}
For distinct odd primes $q_1,\dots,q_k$ and any $\varepsilon \in \{0,1\}^k$ there is a positive integer $N$ with $r(q_i,N) = 2$ when $\varepsilon_i = 1$ and $r(q_i,N) = 0$ when $\varepsilon_i = 0$. Consequently there exist targets with $T_{\Sbase}(N) = \sum_i 2/q_i$ and with $T_{\Sbase}(N) = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each ticket set is nonempty since $(q_i-1)/2 \ge 1$; choose representatives and apply the Chinese remainder theorem, the moduli being pairwise coprime. Root counts depend only on residue classes.
\end{proof}

\section{Optimality: no fit can beat the theory weights}

Let $w \in \R^k$ be arbitrary and set
\[
\widehat T_w(x) = \sum_{i=1}^k w_i \mathbf{1}[x_i \in \Win_i], \qquad \mathcal{R}(w) = \sum_{x \in \Omega} \big(T(x) - \widehat T_w(x)\big)^2 .
\]

\begin{theorem}[Exact second moment]\label{thm:secmom}
For any $w$,
\[
\sum_{x\in\Omega} \widehat T_w(x)^2 = |\Omega|\left[\Big(\sum_i \frac{w_i}{2}\Big)^2 + \sum_i \frac{w_i^2}{4}\right].
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $\widehat T_w = (\widehat T_w - m) + m$ with $m = \sum_i w_i/2$, expand, use $\sum_x(\widehat T_w - m) = 0$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:var} applied to the weights $w$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact risk formula]\label{thm:risk}
Put $\delta_i = 2/q_i - w_i$. Then
\[
\mathcal{R}(w) = |\Omega|\left[\Big(\sum_i \frac{\delta_i}{2}\Big)^2 + \sum_i \frac{\delta_i^2}{4}\right].
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The read-out is linear in the weights, so $T - \widehat T_w = \widehat T_\delta$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:secmom} to $\delta$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique minimiser]\label{thm:unique}
$\mathcal{R}(w) \ge 0$ always, $\mathcal{R}$ vanishes at the theory weights, and $\mathcal{R}(w) = 0$ if and only if $w_i = 2/q_i$ for every $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Non-negativity is clear. At $w_i = 2/q_i$ the difference vanishes identically. Conversely, since $|\Omega| > 0$ and the two bracket terms are separately non-negative, vanishing forces $\sum_i \delta_i^2 = 0$, hence $\delta \equiv 0$.
\end{proof}

The risk landscape over the whole $k$-dimensional weight space is an explicit positive-semidefinite quadratic form in the deviations from theory, with a unique zero at the theory point. Fitting is not merely unnecessary: its global optimum is known in closed form before the search begins, and any vector obtained from finite noisy data lands at strictly positive risk. Note the shape of the bracket --- a mean-squared term plus a variance term --- so deviations that cancel in the mean are still penalised; no direction in weight space is free.

\begin{theorem}[Truncation risk]\label{thm:trunc}
Let $S \subseteq \{1,\dots,k\}$ be the retained index set and let $w_i = 2/q_i$ for $i \in S$ and $w_i = 0$ otherwise. Then
\[
\mathcal{R}(w) = |\Omega|\left[\Big(\sum_{i \notin S} \frac{1}{q_i}\Big)^2 + \sum_{i \notin S} \frac{1}{q_i^2}\right].
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Truncation is strictly costly]\label{thm:truncpos}
If at least one prime is dropped, $\mathcal{R}(w) > 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $j \notin S$ the second bracket term is at least $1/q_j^2 > 0$ and the first is non-negative.
\end{proof}

At the level of the dial itself, for $\Sbase \subseteq \Sbase'$ one has $T_{\Sbase}(N) \le T_{\Sbase'}(N)$ with exact deficit
\[
T_{\Sbase'}(N) - T_{\Sbase}(N) = \sum_{\substack{p \in \Sbase'\setminus\Sbase\\ b(p,N)=1}} \frac{2}{p},
\]
a target-dependent quantity --- which is precisely why truncation degrades a ranking rather than merely shifting it.

\section{Concentration, uniformly in the factor base}

The moments exhibit a structural tension: the mean $\sum_i 1/q_i$ diverges as $B \to \infty$ (Mertens: $\sim \log\log B$) while the variance is absolutely bounded.

\begin{lemma}[Bounded variance]\label{lem:var}
For any finite family of distinct integers $q_i \ge 3$,
\[
\sum_i \frac{1}{q_i^2} \;\le\; \sum_{m \ge 3}\frac{1}{m^2} \;<\; \frac12 .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Distinctness lets us embed the family in $\{3,\dots,M\}$ and dominate term by term; the telescoping bound $1/m^2 < 1/(m-1) - 1/m$ gives $\sum_{m=3}^M 1/m^2 < 1/2 - 1/M$ by induction. No analytic input is needed.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chebyshev, uniform]\label{thm:cheb}
For every $t > 0$,
\[
\frac{\#\{x \in \Omega : |T(x) - M(\Sbase)| \ge t\}}{|\Omega|} \;\le\; \frac{\sum_i 1/q_i^2}{t^2} \;\le\; \frac{1}{2t^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The finite Chebyshev inequality $\#\{|f-m|\ge t\}\,t^2 \le \sum_x (f(x)-m)^2$ with $f = T$, $m = M(\Sbase)$, plus Theorem~\ref{thm:var} and Lemma~\ref{lem:var}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
There always exists $x \in \Omega$ with $|T(x) - M(\Sbase)| < 1$: the Mertens weight is attained up to $1$, for every factor base. Moreover if a fraction at least $c>0$ of targets deviate by at least $t$ then $2ct^2 \le 1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Coin moment generating function]\label{lem:mgf}
For an odd prime $p$ and reals $s,w$, the centred coin $c(y) = w\mathbf{1}[y \in \Win] - w/2$ on $(\Z/p\Z)^\times$ satisfies $\sum_y e^{s c(y)} = (p-1)\cosh(sw/2)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The coin takes the value $+w/2$ on $\Win$ and $-w/2$ on $\Lose$, sets of equal size $(p-1)/2$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:fair}.
\end{proof}

The absence of a first-order term is exactly the fairness of the lottery: an unfair coin would contribute a linear drift and destroy the symmetric $\cosh$.

\begin{lemma}[Factorisation]\label{lem:fact}
For any weights $w$ and any $s \in \R$,
\[
\sum_{x\in\Omega} \exp\Big(s\big(\widehat T_w(x) - \textstyle\sum_i w_i/2\big)\Big) = |\Omega| \prod_{i=1}^k \cosh\Big(\frac{s w_i}{2}\Big).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The centred read-out is a sum of one-coordinate functions, the exponential of a sum is a product, and sums of products of one-coordinate functions over a product set factor into products of one-coordinate sums; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:mgf} coordinatewise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sub-Gaussian bound]\label{thm:subg}
With $V = \sum_i 1/q_i^2$ and any $s \in \R$,
\[
\sum_{x\in\Omega} \exp\big(s(T(x) - M(\Sbase))\big) \le |\Omega|\exp\Big(\frac{s^2}{2}V\Big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:fact} with $w_i = 2/q_i$ gives $\prod_i \cosh(s/q_i)$; apply $\cosh u \le e^{u^2/2}$ termwise and collect exponents.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided Hoeffding tail]\label{thm:tail}
For $t \ge 0$ and $V = \sum_i 1/q_i^2 > 0$,
\[
\#\{x \in \Omega : |T(x) - M(\Sbase)| \ge t\} \;\le\; 2|\Omega| \exp\Big(-\frac{t^2}{2V}\Big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Exponential Markov: for $s \ge 0$, $\#\{T - M \ge t\}e^{st} \le \sum_x e^{s(T-M)} \le |\Omega|e^{s^2V/2}$, so the upper-tail count is at most $|\Omega|\exp(s^2V/2 - st)$; optimise at $s = t/V$. The lower tail follows by the symmetry $s \mapsto -s$ of $\cosh$; add.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform sub-Gaussian tail]\label{thm:unif}
For distinct odd primes and every $t \ge 0$,
\[
\frac{\#\{x \in \Omega : |T(x) - M(\Sbase)| \ge t\}}{|\Omega|} \le 2e^{-t^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:tail} with $V \le 1/2$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:var}); the degenerate cases $k=0$ and $t=0$ are direct.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
$2e^{-4} < 1/(2\cdot 2^2)$, i.e.\ $0.0366\ldots < 0.125$: the exponential bound beats the polynomial one already at $t = 2$. Moreover, whenever $2e^{-t^2} < 1$ --- in particular for every $t \ge 1$ --- some target reads within $t$ of the Mertens weight.
\end{proposition}

As the factor base grows, the centre of the dial's distribution drifts upward without bound, but the shape around that centre is frozen: a sub-Gaussian profile with variance proxy at most $1/2$, identical for all factor bases.

\section{The position side: a dual reading}

Let $\Pi = \prod_i \Z/q_i\Z$ be the space of sieve positions modulo $Q$ and, for fixed $N$, define the hit counter
\[
H(x) = \#\{i : q_i \mid x^2 - N\} = \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbf{1}[x_i \in R(q_i,N)] .
\]

\begin{theorem}[Position-side mean]
$\E_\Pi[H] = \sum_{i=1}^k r(q_i,N)/q_i$, which equals $T_{\Sbase}(N)$ when $\Sbase$ is admissible for $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Position-side variance]
With $r_i = r(q_i,N)$,
\[
\Var_\Pi[H] = \sum_{i=1}^k \frac{r_i}{q_i}\Big(1 - \frac{r_i}{q_i}\Big)
= \sum_{i \,:\, b(q_i,N)=1} \frac{2}{q_i}\Big(1 - \frac{2}{q_i}\Big)
\]
for admissible $\Sbase$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Centre each indicator and expand: diagonal terms give the Bernoulli variances and cross terms vanish by two-coordinate factorisation over the product space $\Pi$.
\end{proof}

The hit counter is thus a sum of independent Bernoulli indicators with theory-forced success probabilities $r_i/q_i \in \{0, 2/q_i\}$; its mean is the dial. The agreement of the two readings --- across targets and across positions --- is a consistency check on the framework.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

\paragraph{Dial evaluation.} Given $N$ and $B$: sieve the odd primes $p \le B$ once ($O(B\log\log B)$, cached); for each $p$, add $1/p$ if $p \mid N$, else compute the Legendre symbol by binary reciprocity in $O(\log^2 p)$ bit operations and add $2/p$ when it is $+1$. Total $O(\pi(B)\log^2 B)$ --- microseconds for $B = 400$, negligible beside any factoring attempt. The dial is a free pre-screen.

\paragraph{Exact distribution.} Mean $\sum 1/q_i$ and variance $\sum 1/q_i^2$ are $O(k)$. The full law is that of $\sum_i (2/q_i)\mathrm{Bern}(1/2)$, a $2^k$-atom distribution on $[0, 2M]$, tabulable to accuracy $\epsilon$ in $O(k/\epsilon)$ by discretised convolution.

\paragraph{Risk audit.} For a proposed weight vector $w$, return $(\sum_i \delta_i/2)^2 + \sum_i \delta_i^2/4$ with $\delta_i = 2/q_i - w_i$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:risk}), in $O(k)$: any fitting procedure can be audited in linear time against the closed-form optimum, without cross-validation.

\paragraph{Target steering.} For a desired pattern $\varepsilon$, find per-prime residues with the prescribed character by Euler's criterion (expected $O(1)$ trials each, since half the classes qualify) and combine by CRT in $O(k \log^2 Q)$; Theorem~\ref{thm:steer} guarantees success.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{What is established.} The dial is exactly the expected per-position factor-base footprint, with $2/p$ forced by the root-count identity; it is a function of a sufficient statistic, so no per-prime measurement improves it; it is the unique minimiser of squared error over all linear read-outs, with an explicit quadratic penalty for every deviation and a strictly positive penalty for every truncation; and it is a sum of exactly independent fair coins with diverging mean and absolutely bounded variance, hence sub-Gaussian with a factor-base-free tail. The observed superiority of the zero-parameter dial over an eight-parameter fitted model is therefore not a small-sample accident: every fitted alternative is strictly worse against the true footprint, and the only way for a fit to approach the dial's accuracy is to converge to the dial.

\paragraph{What is not established.} The theorems concern the footprint, not the smoothness \emph{yield} that a factoring run ultimately cares about; the passage from one to the other involves a Dickman-type smoothness density and is not linear, and the empirical scale factor together with the residual unexplained variance live exactly in that gap. The distributional statements use the uniform measure on $\Omega$, which is the correct null model but is a null model: a real workload of targets need not be uniform. Finally, the bound $V < 1/2$ is uniform but not tight in practice: for the odd primes up to $400$, $V \approx 0.2019$, giving the sharper tail $2\exp(-t^2/0.4038) \approx 2e^{-2.48 t^2}$; $V$ is dominated by its first terms, so it is essentially constant once $B$ exceeds a few hundred.

\paragraph{Relation to classical sieve theory.} The Mertens decomposition places the dial in a familiar frame: a standard sieve density constant plus a character sum over primes of the type controlled by large-sieve and (conditionally) GRH bounds. Our results give an unconditional exact-distribution account of the fluctuation term for a fixed factor base --- a statement about the distribution over $N$, complementary to classical statements about individual $N$. Sieve analyses routinely \emph{assume} independence across primes and \emph{approximate} the hit density by $2/p$; here both are theorems with no error term, so the remaining heuristic content of a sieve analysis is isolated in the smoothness step.

\paragraph{Methodological reading.} Whenever a modelling problem admits a sufficient discrete statistic and a linear read-out, the fitting exercise collapses to identifying the coefficient forced by the underlying structure. The right question is not which coefficients fit best, but what identity determines them.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Beyond the footprint: yield.} Relate the dial to smoothness yield by combining the position-side Bernoulli structure with a Dickman/Buchstab analysis of the residual cofactor, aiming at an asymptotic or a rigorous monotonicity statement in $T$.
\item \textbf{Higher moments and Poisson limits.} The moment generating function factorisation gives all cumulants in closed form from $\log\cosh(s/q_i)$; extracting a (compound) Poisson limit for the hit counter as $B \to \infty$ looks tractable.
\item \textbf{Non-uniform target ensembles.} Replace the uniform measure by one reflecting an actual workload (e.g.\ semiprimes of fixed bit length) and determine whether the bit vector remains uniform --- an equidistribution question with a large-sieve flavour.
\item \textbf{Character-sum sharpening.} Combine exact distributional control over $N$ with classical bounds for fixed $N$ to obtain hybrid statements, e.g.\ counting $N \le X$ whose dial exceeds the Mertens weight by more than $t$.
\item \textbf{Other sieves.} For multiple-polynomial variants with $f(x) = ax^2+bx+c$ the same root-count identity holds with the Legendre symbol of the discriminant; for the number field sieve the analogue counts degree-one primes above $p$. Both should admit a zero-fit dial with the coefficient forced by the same mechanism.
\item \textbf{Steering as a design tool.} Every bit pattern is realisable, so benchmark construction, best- and worst-case instance generation, and multiplier selection (choose small $m$ maximising $T(mN)$) become concrete finite optimisations.
\item \textbf{Nonlinear read-outs.} Optimality is established within the linear class. If the target is yield rather than footprint, the optimal read-out of the (still sufficient) bit vector may be nonlinear; characterising it is the natural next question.
\end{itemize}

